<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The mark: two discs, overlapping, and the place where they overlap is brighter
  than either of them.

  That is the product in one shape. Two people, one shared space, and the shared
  space is the point — not a heart, which every couple app already uses and which
  says nothing about what this one does.

  Why a vector and not a PNG: it is exact at every density, it is readable as
  source, and changing the accent pair later is an edit to two hex values rather
  than a regeneration of ten binary files.

  Geometry. Adaptive icons are a 108x108 viewport of which the launcher may mask
  anything outside the centre 66dp — the outer ring is used for parallax and
  shape masking and cannot be relied on. Both discs have r=22 at y=54, centred
  at x=43 and x=65, so the mark spans x=21 to x=87: exactly 66dp, filling the
  safe zone without touching the part that gets cropped.
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <!-- Coral. One partner. -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M21,54 a22,22 0 1,0 44,0 a22,22 0 1,0 -44,0"
        android:fillColor="#e86c46" />

    <!-- Iris. The other. 112 degrees away on the wheel and a different lightness,
         so the two stay distinguishable to a colourblind eye — the same rule the
         twelve accents in packages/core are generated under. -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M43,54 a22,22 0 1,0 44,0 a22,22 0 1,0 -44,0"
        android:fillColor="#986ce5" />

    <!--
      The overlap, drawn as its own lens rather than left to a blend mode —
      vector drawables have no blend modes, and relying on alpha would make the
      shared area darker than its parents rather than brighter.

      The colour is the screen blend of the two accents, computed rather than
      picked: 1-(1-a)(1-b) per channel. Light is what you get when both are
      present.
    -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M54,34.95 A22,22 0 0,1 54,73.05 A22,22 0 0,1 54,34.95 Z"
        android:fillColor="#f6a9ec" />
</vector>
